  <!-- ================= Testimonials ================= -->
  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="voices-title" style="padding-top: 0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="voices" id="testimonials">
        <div class="voices-head sec-head rv">
          <p class="eyebrow">Testimonials</p>
          <h2 id="voices-title">In their own words</h2>
          <p class="sec-sub">Written when the technique was called thoughtfeel.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="voices-nav" hidden>
          <button type="button" class="voices-arrow" data-dir="-1" aria-label="Earlier testimonials">‹</button>
          <button type="button" class="voices-arrow" data-dir="1" aria-label="More testimonials">›</button>
        </div>
        <div class="voices-track" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Testimonials, side by side">
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <img class="voice-photo" src="/img/julian-putnam.jpg" width="88" height="88" alt="" loading="lazy">
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Julian Putnam</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Data analyst</span>
          <a class="voice-link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I3WgK8cPa0A" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch his first lesson</a>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“This brand new information, on a topic I knew nothing about going in, still feels so consolidated and easily accessible the morning after.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>Ted and I go way back to our high school days, so naturally, I was skeptical of anything he was proclaiming to invent or teach. But over the years, I’ve seen firsthand his dedication to building, researching, developing, and surprising everyone with what he decides to do next.</p>
          <p>This time it’s thoughtfeel: a technique that identifies and names the ability to think in concept bubbles as a new sense, with a gentle learning curve. After my lesson, I feel like I understand how it can help people remember way more, for much longer.</p>
          <p>We recorded the lesson from around 7 to 9 pm, as Ted walked me through a story about TD learning in thoughtfeel. I had no knowledge of TD learning going in. After the lesson, I didn’t put any more effort into remembering the story, and I haven’t researched it since. The next morning, I could still lay it out. The learner goes through trial after trial, and its prediction is designed to approach the reward, moment after moment. Each trial has a tone moment and a wait moment. The prediction error is the reward, plus the next tone moment’s prediction, minus this moment’s prediction: on the first trial, 1 + 0 − 0 = 1. Then the prediction moves by the learning rate, 0.5, times the error, so it becomes 0.5.</p>
          <p>I’m most surprised at how this brand new information, on a topic I knew nothing about going in, still feels so consolidated and easily accessible the morning after.</p>
          <p>It felt weird at first to dial in my brain to think in this way, but my brain was able to adjust quickly, and the payoff is crystal clear. I recommend anyone who’s curious about what their mind is capable of to give this a try.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <img class="voice-photo" src="/img/rohan-reddy.jpg" width="88" height="88" alt="" loading="lazy">
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Rohan Reddy</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Second-year medical student</span>
          <a class="voice-link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HehmkEjRHYc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch his lesson</a>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“I was able to retain a conceptual story of something I had learned once and give a coherent explanation on my first try.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>I spent an hour with Ted learning about Bloom filters and building the entire story in thoughtfeel. I’d tried thoughtfeel before, building a story about iron-deficiency anemia. It definitely worked at the time, but it felt like a slow and grueling process to build the concepts.</p>
          <p>In our session, Ted challenged me to build each part of the story as it went, without repeating or stopping between parts. His reasoning was that stopping to read the line and then build it increases the cognitive load. I took on the challenge, and I remembered much more at the end than I would have thought. I realized it was my anxiety that made me want to go slow and build everything until I felt sure about it. My advice is to trust yourself and push through that feeling. The goal is to build as you go. It’s way faster and feels more fun!</p>
          <p>The next day, without revisiting it, I wrote out everything I could recall about Bloom filters: how each item gets three hashes that turn three bits to one, how the filter checks an item against those bits, and how an item that was never added can still match, which is a false positive. Then I asked an AI chat where the gaps in my understanding were, rewrote those parts as direct, simple sentences, and rebuilt them in thoughtfeel.</p>
          <p>Will you become a genius who remembers everything on the first try? It’s a possibility! Unfortunately, that is not me as of now 😅. But I was able to retain a conceptual story of something I had learned once and give a coherent explanation on my first try, and I’m impressed. thoughtfeel is a mental exercise, and like anything in life, if you put in dedicated practice, you will get better. From our session, I learned I had been building incorrectly, and that’s why I was seeing minimal results.</p>
          <p>I would bet many people reading this are like me. When I watch a lecture, I’m mostly listening to the voice or reading the slide, or taking notes and trying to recreate the story on paper. I’m not normally aware of what’s going on in my head. With thoughtfeel, I’m learning directly in my mind. I can see, but it’s not necessarily a “visual” process.</p>
          <p>For the next few months, I’ll be building my medical school lectures in thoughtfeel and seeing where it takes me. If you’ve been experimenting with thoughtfeel and found some benefit, I highly recommend going through a learning session with Ted.</p>
          <p>As a disclaimer, I’ve known Ted for more than a year. We met because I was curious about an early version of his work, from before thoughtfeel. Ted is a highly passionate and driven person, and he pushes me to believe that I, like anyone reading this, have a greater capacity for learning and memorizing than I may have believed.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <img class="voice-photo" src="/img/liam-daly-smith.jpg" width="88" height="88" alt="" loading="lazy">
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Liam Daly-Smith</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Environmental engineering master’s student</span>
          <a class="voice-link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zriqAnYCMZs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch his lesson</a>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“It felt like assembling a machine out of concepts, and I could hold the numbers and ideas in my head with ease.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>I’ve been using thoughtfeel on and off, and sometimes found it hard to use. A lot of the difficulty came down to my ADHD. I often run into auditory processing delays, and I can struggle to turn what I hear into meaningful thoughts. With thoughtfeel, that meant the audio stories were causing me a lot of trouble.</p>
          <p>When I tried using visuals and reading the lines instead, it was like flipping a switch. I was immediately able to build the Bloom filter in thoughtfeel, seamlessly. It felt like assembling a machine out of concepts, and I could hold the numbers and ideas in my head with ease.</p>
          <p>I want to share my experience as a neurodivergent thinker, so others know: if you’re having trouble using thoughtfeel, try different approaches. It’s honestly really awesome being able to use it in full, and I’m really glad I gave this version a try.</p>
          <p>Building it felt like creating thought machinery in my head, then stamping helpful visuals on top of it. Now, whenever I go to access it, I find a bird’s-eye view of this concept machine with key visuals stamped on it, almost like dictionary keys in a data structure, and boom, I can find the meaning of the concept immediately. It’s honestly super cool, and I’m looking forward to using it to learn more.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <img class="voice-photo" src="/img/zed-barakat.jpg" width="88" height="88" alt="" loading="lazy">
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Khaled “Zed” Barakat</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Third-year medical student</span>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“I understood the material before. Now I am learning how to recall it.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>I am Khaled Barakat but most people know me as Zed. I am a third year medical student diagnosed with ADHD. I’ve always been able to understand concepts fairly quickly, but memorizing and recalling details has been a different story. I used to spend hours reading, repeating, and writing things down only to sometimes get stuck while recalling because I forgot one detail or I couldn’t remember the right sequence.</p>
          <p>I came across Ted’s post about Thoughtfeel and decided to give it a shot. We used glycolysis as a test during our call. We built connected concepts around what was actually happening at each step instead of just memorizing everything in the process as a list. I managed to recall the entire pathway in less than five minutes after trying it again after our call.</p>
          <p>Ted then introduced me to Dijkstra’s algorithm. Dijkstra’s algorithm was something completely new to me and outside my field. I initially couldn’t make sense of how it worked. Ted then walked me through it using Thoughtfeel. To my surprise, I was able to follow the process and execute it myself. I tested my recall the next morning on the routes that we built, and it felt as easy to remember as my own name.</p>
          <p>That got me curious about applying it to medicine. So, I started using Thoughtfeel with neuroanatomy and neuropathologies and tumors, and I’ve started noticing improvements in both recall and the time it takes me to precisely recall information and solve practice questions.</p>
          <p>I’m still learning how to build bubbles efficiently especially when trying to keep up with a live lecture, but I haven’t tested my exam performance or long-term retention yet so there’s still a lot for me to explore.</p>
          <p>What stood out to me most was being able to work with information rather than just trying to hold on to it. I’m genuinely excited to see where I can take this.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <img class="voice-photo" src="/img/max-gurkalov.jpg" width="88" height="88" alt="" loading="lazy">
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Max Gurkalov</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Learning Japanese and QA engineering</span>
          <a class="voice-link" href="https://www.youtube.com/@Weiming_YT" target="_blank" rel="noopener">You can find him on YouTube</a>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“30 seconds per character, once, versus ~30 minutes of daily Anki reviews with low retention.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>Hello! I’ve been learning to use thoughtfeel ever since you started talking about it, and I want to share my results, because they’re kind of insane to me.</p>
          <p>I’m learning Japanese, and I’ve been using thoughtfeel for kanji. I break a character into parts that each produce a thoughtfeel, connect them together, then connect that to the reading and the meaning. Take <span lang="ja">富</span>. On its own, it didn’t produce any thoughtfeel. But split into elements (4 boxes that look like a drawer, a rectangle on top, a line on top and a thing that looks like a lid), each part produced its own thoughtfeel – so I connected them. Then I attached the reading, “tomi”, and the meaning, “wealth”. The whole thing took about 30 seconds.</p>
          <p>The crazy part is that it got ingrained right away and I can remember very clearly all of the ones I learned. I recognize them in text, I can write them out from memory, and the written form, sound and meaning feel as connected as any word in English or in my native language (Russian).</p>
          <p>For comparison, I used to study Chinese in the past (without success) with the currently recommended method: take words in context, put them into Anki, do active recall on reading and meaning of the characters. I “learned” around 200 characters that way, and I couldn’t recognize them while reading, let alone write them. I remember exactly 2 of them: <span lang="zh">一</span> (yī) and <span lang="zh">了</span> (le). I think my brain relied on the context of the flashcard instead of actually memorizing the characters.</p>
          <p>So it’s 30 seconds per character, once, versus ~30 minutes of daily Anki reviews with low retention. It’s just insane how efficient thoughtfeel is.</p>
          <p>And it’s not only kanji. Right now, I’m using it to study for QA engineering from scratch. TF makes it much easier to understand the lessons and I can now recite them forwards and backwards without any rehearsal. I also use it to create reminders for myself throughout the day, to learn lessons from books and videos, to memorize useful discussions in conversations, to remember some insights and ideas that I came up with and for general thinking.</p>
          <p>It’s basically the endgame for studying and mnemonic techniques. Thank you!</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <img class="voice-photo" src="/img/matthew-sinclair.jpg" width="88" height="88" alt="" loading="lazy">
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Matthew Sinclair</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Software engineering leader with aphantasia</span>
          <a class="voice-link" href="https://matthewsinclair.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">matthewsinclair.com</a>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“It works with the way my mind already works, instead of asking it to do something it can’t.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>I have aphantasia: I can’t form mental images, and I have no inner voice. When I think of something, say a chair, I don’t see a chair. I get the concept of a chair, which is hard to describe to anyone who thinks in pictures. The best I can do is that my thinking is made of clumps of concepts, linked to one another in a kind of graph of relationships. It’s elusive, like something at the corner of your eye that disappears when you turn to look at it.</p>
          <p>That has always made rote memory hard for me. I can’t call up a picture of what I’ve learned, so I have to understand something well enough to build an intuition for it. Visual techniques like the memory palace just don’t work for me: there’s no palace to walk through. The closest I came was mind maps, through my computer science degree and my MBA, and even those I was trying to remember as an image.</p>
          <p>What drew me to ThoughtFeel was the language in Ted’s videos. He described thinking in words very close to the ones I’ve used for years to explain how I think about my own thinking. So I tried it on his list, Anchor, Saffron, Bicycle, Grumble. Normally I’d never remember a list like that. With ThoughtFeel it stuck, and 24 hours later I could still run the chain of concepts easily, forwards and backwards.</p>
          <p>For me the difference from visual mnemonics is that I’m not translating anything into a picture. I’m linking the concepts themselves, and saying each word out loud seems to tie the sound to the concept. It works with the way my mind already works, instead of asking it to do something it can’t.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <span class="voice-photo voice-initial" aria-hidden="true">F</span>
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Fred</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Co-founder of a health tech startup, learning Dutch</span>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“I didn’t have to force an image into my mind. I could just grasp the concept and understand what it meant.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>When I first spoke with Ted about ThoughtFeel, I realised how much I had been relying on visualisation to learn and remember things. I was used to having to picture things in my head, and that could get tiring, especially when learning something new.</p>
          <p>Trying ThoughtFeel felt different. I didn’t have to force an image into my mind. I could just grasp the concept and understand what it meant.</p>
          <p>I’ve been using it to learn Dutch because I need the language for my startup in the Netherlands, and honestly, learning feels less stressful and more natural. I find it easier to grasp things and recall what I’ve learned. It’s been a really interesting experience because I never thought I could learn this way.</p>
          <p>Thank you for introducing me to ThoughtFeel Ted! Forever grateful</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <span class="voice-photo voice-initial" aria-hidden="true">H</span>
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Henry S.</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Montclair, NJ</span>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“Having the language to describe this kind of thinking is completely revolutionary, and I hope it can help a lot of other people.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>I have always felt that most of my thoughts have a significant portion that are not tied to the five senses. The technique isn’t completely new to me, but I’ve never had the words to describe it before. In the past I would make oblique connections between things. It helps a lot with understanding things, like the underlying machinery behind some math concepts, and also makes picking up new games easier. The downside is that it makes explaining things difficult, which is where the idea of thoughtfeel comes in handy. Having the language to describe this kind of thinking is completely revolutionary, and I hope it can help a lot of other people.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
      <figure class="voice">
      <figcaption class="voice-who">
        <span class="voice-photo voice-initial" aria-hidden="true">A</span>
        <span class="voice-meta">
          <span class="voice-name">Anonymous</span>
          <span class="voice-role">Watched the 14-minute Bloom filter lesson</span>
          <a class="voice-link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tgLRhlMjVkI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the lesson</a>
        </span>
      </figcaption>
      <p class="voice-pull">“If I’d had access to this type of learning 15 years ago, my career choice &amp; life path may have played out differently.”</p>
      <details class="voice-more">
        <summary><span class="voice-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="voice-closed">Read the whole message</span><span class="voice-open">Show less</span></summary>
        <blockquote class="voice-text">
          <p>To start, my head has never felt so clear while learning from a video/audio source. I generally only find a “flow” to learning that feels this way when alone, at my own pace, reading from text I can reread if needed, and with blank paper beside me for notes/questions/connective ideas to other topics.</p>
          <p>I could be wrong, but if I’d had access to this type of learning 15 years ago, my career choice &amp; life path may have played out differently.</p>
          <p>The bloom filter vid didn’t require notes and I only replayed sections to be sure I was understanding correctly, not because of confusion. The end bit, where you addressed how the lesson might normally be taught, was what really drove home for me how successful the approach actually was. I’d totally forgotten how much of a struggle learning concepts can be when approaching them from a traditional perspective.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </details>
    </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= The definition: one sentence, then parts that open ================= -->
  <section class="sec what" id="what" aria-labelledby="what-title" style="padding-top: 0">
    <div class="wrap what-grid">
      <div class="what-head rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">The technique</p>
        <h2 id="what-title">What are concept bubbles?</h2>
        <p class="what-lead">Concept bubbles is a learning technique. A concept bubble is one idea, held in your mind with no words and no picture. You take in the idea and its properties all at once, the way vision lets you take in what’s in front of you.</p>
        <p class="what-more">Open any part to read more. The <a href="/concept-bubbles/">definition page</a> has the rest.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="what-parts rv">
        <details class="what-part">
          <summary>
            <span class="what-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false"><path d="M2.5 12S6 5.5 12 5.5 21.5 12 21.5 12 18 18.5 12 18.5 2.5 12 2.5 12z"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/></svg></span>
            <h3 id="what-sense">Like vision, but for concepts</h3><span class="what-gist">Vision gives you a dog’s fur, nose and size at once. A concept bubble does that for something like the economy.</span>
            <span class="what-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <div class="what-part-body">
            <p>You could describe a dog in writing: it has fur, a wet black nose, and it’s about the size of a capybara. Or you could look at it, and vision gives you all of that at once, with no list.</p>
            <p>Concepts have properties too, including properties you could never see. Take the economy. You could write out what’s true of it: millions of people, making and trading things, sometimes booming and sometimes in a slump. People who use concept bubbles can still make that list in words. But in a concept bubble, properties like these are taken in all at once.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="what-part">
          <summary>
            <span class="what-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false"><circle cx="9" cy="12" r="5.5"/><circle cx="15" cy="12" r="5.5"/></svg></span>
            <h3 id="what-compare">Spotting what’s different</h3><span class="what-gist">Two concepts that aren’t the same are clearly different right away, like photos of two dogs.</span>
            <span class="what-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <div class="what-part-body">
            <p>That makes comparing fast. Show someone photos of two dogs, and they can tell right away whether it’s the same dog. If it isn’t, they can look again and point to why: one has a brown tail, the other a white one. With concept bubbles, the same works for concepts you imagine. If two concepts aren’t the same, that’s clear right away, and paying attention to each one shows which properties differ.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="what-part">
          <summary>
            <span class="what-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false"><path d="M5 19v-3M10 19v-7M15 19V9M20 19V5"/></svg></span>
            <h3 id="what-range">Faint, vivid, or not at all</h3><span class="what-gist">About 73% of people have some unsymbolized thinking. It ranges from faint to vivid, the way the mind’s eye does.</span>
            <span class="what-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <div class="what-part-body">
            <p>Concept bubbles use unsymbolized thinking, and not everyone has it. Like vision and hearing, it differs from person to person, and it most likely varies the way the mind’s eye does. Based on studies of unsymbolized thinking, about 73% of people have some of it, some have none, and among those who have it, it ranges from faint to vivid. For some people, taking in and imagining concepts is the default way they experience the world. For others, it’s much quieter. Just as people can imagine in pictures or in sounds, people with unsymbolized thinking can imagine in concepts.</p>
            <p class="what-cite">Source: Russell Hurlburt’s study of inner experience (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.concog.2007.12.006" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heavey &amp; Hurlburt, 2008</a>). 22 of 30 people had unsymbolized thinking at least once: a specific thought that comes without words or pictures. Hurlburt named and studies unsymbolized thinking. The concept bubbles technique is Ted Shachtman’s, and Hurlburt isn’t involved in it.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="what-part">
          <summary>
            <span class="what-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false"><path d="M13 2.5 5 13.5h6l-1 8 8-11h-6l1-8z"/></svg></span>
            <h3 id="what-once">Is it really all at once?</h3><span class="what-gist">Taking in the concept is. Putting its properties into words comes after.</span>
            <span class="what-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <div class="what-part-body">
            <p>Taking in the concept is. Putting its properties into words comes after. When a friend walks into a room, you know it’s them right away, before you could describe a single feature. If someone asks what color their eyes are, you look again and say. A concept bubble works the same way. Someone who holds a promise as a concept bubble knows right away what it is. Then they can pay attention to it and put its properties into words: it’s made to someone, it’s about the future, and it can be broken.</p>
            <p>With practice, people notice finer properties, the way a birdwatcher learns to spot details in a bird that look the same to everyone else.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="what-part" id="why-it-works">
          <summary>
            <span class="what-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false"><path d="M5 4.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 6.5 3H19v15H6.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 0 5 19.5z"/><path d="M5 19.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 0 6.5 21H19"/><path d="M9 7.5h6M9 11h4"/></svg></span>
            <h3 id="what-why">Why concept bubbles work: follow a conceptual story the way your mind’s eye helps you follow a fairy tale</h3><span class="what-gist">The four steps, side by side, in one picture.</span>
            <span class="what-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <div class="what-part-body">
            <p>If I tell you, “A boy and a girl went to the well. The boy pumped water, and water came out of the well. The girl took a bucket and poured the water on a patch of green grass,” you can understand and remember that story better by using your mind’s eye to imagine it.</p>
            <p>But if I tell you, “The mother of the regent of the land is intrigued by the prospect of discovery,” you can’t use your mind’s eye to follow along, because the story is conceptual, not visual.</p>
            <p>With concept bubbles, though, you can use your ability to imagine in concepts to build that story in your imagination. That helps you understand the conceptual story the same way your mind’s eye helps you understand the visual one.</p>
            <figure class="what-figure">
              <a href="/img/two-stories.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                <picture>
                  <source srcset="/img/two-stories.webp" type="image/webp">
                  <img src="/img/two-stories.jpg" width="1456" height="1900" loading="lazy" alt="Two columns, the same four steps. A visual story about things you can see: read the words, choose your mind’s eye, picture each line, and end up with a movie in your head. A conceptual story about things you can’t see or touch: read the words, choose unsymbolized thinking, imagine each line as a concept bubble, and end up with a concept movie. Both end the same way: you understand what happened, and you remember it long-term.">
                </picture>
              </a>
              <figcaption>A visual story and a conceptual story, built the same way. <a href="/img/two-stories.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it full size</a></figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="what-part">
          <summary>
            <span class="what-icon" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="6.5"/><path d="M12 2.5v3M12 18.5v3M2.5 12h3M18.5 12h3M5.3 5.3l2.1 2.1M16.6 16.6l2.1 2.1M5.3 18.7l2.1-2.1M16.6 7.4l2.1-2.1"/></svg></span>
            <h3 id="what-machine">Running a concept like a machine</h3><span class="what-gist">Build an algorithm or a biological process in concepts, change one property, and see what it does.</span>
            <span class="what-plus" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <div class="what-part-body">
            <p>Concept bubbles also help with mental simulation of concepts, the same way that your mind’s eye helps with mental simulation of objects. If you want to figure out what happens when you turn a gear in a gearbox, you can use your mind’s eye to run the simulation. With concept bubbles, when you’ve built a conceptual machine like an algorithm or a biological process, you can simulate how the system would behave if you changed a conceptual property.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

